1- lers understand recall@k:

recall@k is a metric used to evaluate performance of recommendation systems, search engines, 
and information retreival models

it measures the propotions of relevent items that are successfully retrieved withint the top K results

formula:

    Recall@K = (Number of relevant items in top K results) / (Total number of relevant items)



suppose you have 5 relevent items in to K results
and your system returned 5 documnts, 3 of them are relevant and other 2 are uselss

so 3/5 that 60% accuracy!

Recall@5 = 3 / 5 = 0.6 (60%)


--> it measure the relevant items:
    of all files that should be returned how many of them have been found, 
    how many did we actually find??



the difference between recall@k and precision@k?


precision@k:   how many of the top K results are relevant? precision
recall@k:      how many relevent files made it to top k?  coverage

increasing K usually improves recall and hurt precision!!



recall@k is used when we missing a docuemnt is costly
medical research, legal discovery, condidate sourcing

--> also when you have a re-ranker or LLM that can filter, better to use recall@k
--> and let the LLM filter irrelevant info!
























